## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming analytics under fixed memory, focusing on real-time percentile estimation and how rebinding affects accuracy and cost.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms, fixed memory, boundary recomputation, percentile estimation, amortized analysis.
- Trade-offs between K, R, and accuracy; cache-friendly bucket access.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Histogram85 {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M; this.K = K;
    this.bounds = Array.from({length: K}, (_,i)=> Math.floor((i+1)*M/K));
    this.counts = new Array(K).fill(0);
  }
  update(x){
    let bucket = Math.min(this.K-1, Math.floor(x/ (this.M/this.K)));
    this.counts[bucket]++;
  }
  total(){ return this.counts.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0); }
  percentile(p){ // p in [0,1]
    const total = this.total();
    const target = p * total;
    let acc = 0;
    for(let i=this.K-1; i>=0; i--){
      acc += this.counts[i];
      if(acc >= target) return this.bounds[i-1] !== undefined ? this.bounds[i-1] : this.M;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose K and R for skewed data?
- How does this scale with M and batch size B?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of sliding-window streaming algorithms, mergeable sketches, and amortized cost analysis under tight memory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding-window distinct counting
- HyperLogLog mergeability
- Bucketization for windowing
- Memory vs error trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingHLL {
  constructor(K, m) {
    this.hlls = Array.from({length: K}, () => new HyperLogLog(m));
    this.idx = 0;
  }
  update(batch) {
    const i = this.idx % this.hlls.length;
    for (const id of batch) this.hlls[i].add(id);
    this.idx++;
  }
  estimate() {
    const merged = HyperLogLog.merge(this.hlls);
    return merged.estimate();
  }
}
```

